-- title:
--   Distinct points of the scaled lattice are at maximum-norm distance at least the mesh
-- statement:
--   Let $h > 0$ and let $w_1, w_2 \in \mathbb{Z}^d$ be two **distinct** points of the integer lattice
--   $$\mathbb{Z}^d \;=\; \{x \in \mathbb{R}^d : \text{every coordinate } x_i \text{ is an integer}\}.$$
--   Then their images in the rescaled lattice $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ are separated by at least $h$ in the maximum norm:
--   $$h \;\le\; \|h w_1 - h w_2\|_\infty,
--   \qquad\text{where}\quad \|x\|_\infty = \sup_i |x_i| .$$
--
--   This is the quantitative statement that the mesh-$h$ lattice is $h$-separated, which is what makes the lattice discrete and locally finite and what underlies every counting argument on it: two distinct lattice sites can never lie in a cube of side smaller than $h$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The lattice is defined as the set of points all of whose coordinates are integer multiples of $1$, so it is a subset of $\mathbb{R}^d$ rather than a subgroup type; distinctness $w_1 \neq w_2$ is distinctness in $\mathbb{R}^d$.
